    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Virtual nodes",
      "question": "Why is a ring with few servers unbalanced, and how do virtual nodes fix it?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. With only a few servers, why is the ring often unbalanced, and how do virtual nodes fix it? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Why is a small ring <em>unbalanced</em>, and how do <em>virtual nodes</em> help?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "ring",
          "say": "With three servers, positions land wherever the hash puts them. Here, A owns almost the whole ring: ten of the twelve keys. That is a hot spot. The fix is virtual nodes. Each physical server is hashed many times, under different names, so it owns many small arcs instead of one big one. As more virtual nodes join, the load evens out, at four keys each. And when a server fails, its arcs are scattered, so its keys spread across the survivors, instead of piling onto one server.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Many small arcs, <em>even load</em>",
