        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Mod N hashing remaps almost every key when N changes. Consistent hashing puts servers and keys on one ring, and each key belongs to the next server clockwise. Adding or removing a server moves only one arc. And virtual nodes balance the load, and spread a failed server's keys. That is why Dynamo and Cassandra both partition data this way.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Consistent hashing, <em>in four lines</em>",
